### Step 1: Identify the magnetic fields due to each magnet at point P

The point P is midway between the two magnets. The total separation is 10 cm, so the distance from the center of each magnet to point P is \(r = 10 \text{ cm} / 2 = 5 \text{ cm} = 0.05 \text{ m}\).
For the horizontal magnet, point P is on its axial line. The magnetic field \(B_1\) due to a magnet at an axial point is given by \(B_{\text{axial}} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} \frac{2M}{r^3}\).
For the vertical magnet, point P is on its equatorial line. The magnetic field \(B_2\) due to a magnet at an equatorial point is given by \(B_{\text{equatorial}} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} \frac{M}{r^3}\).

### Step 2: Determine the net magnetic field

Since the axes of the magnets are perpendicular, the magnetic fields \(B_1\) and \(B_2\) at point P will also be perpendicular to each other. The net magnetic field \(B_{\text{net}}\) is the vector sum of \(B_1\) and \(B_2\).
B_{\text{net}} = \sqrt{B_1^2 + B_2^2}
B_{\text{net}} = \sqrt{\left(\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} \frac{2M}{r^3}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} \frac{M}{r^3}\right)^2}
B_{\text{net}} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} \frac{M}{r^3} \sqrt{2^2 + 1^2} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} \frac{M}{r^3} \sqrt{5}

### Step 3: Substitute the given values and calculate

Given: Dipole moment \(M = 3\sqrt{5}\) J/T, \(r = 0.05\) m, and \(\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi} = 10^{-7}\) Tm/A.
B_{\text{net}} = (10^{-7}) \times \frac{3\sqrt{5}}{(0.05)^3} \times \sqrt{5}
B_{\text{net}} = 10^{-7} \times \frac{3 \times 5}{(5 \times 10^{-2})^3}
B_{\text{net}} = 10^{-7} \times \frac{15}{125 \times 10^{-6}}
B_{\text{net}} = 10^{-7} \times \frac{3}{25 \times 10^{-6}}
B_{\text{net}} = \frac{3}{25} \times 10^{-1} = 0.12 \times 10^{-1} = 0.012 \text{ T}
The magnetic field is \(0.012 \text{ T}\), which can be written as \(12 \times 10^{-3} \text{ T}\).

### Step 4: Find the value of \(\alpha\)

We are given that the magnetic field at point P is \(\alpha \times 10^{-3}\) T. Comparing this with our calculated value:
\alpha \times 10^{-3} \text{ T} = 12 \times 10^{-3} \text{ T}
\alpha = 12
The value of \(\alpha\) is 12.

Given values are:
• Magnetic intensity, \(H = 2.4 \times 10^3 \text{ A/m}\)
• Length of the solenoid, \(\ell = 15 \text{ cm} = 15 \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}\)
• Number of turns, \(N = 60\)
The magnetic intensity \(H\) inside a solenoid is given by:
H = nI
where \(n\) is the number of turns per unit length, given by \(n = \frac{N}{\ell}\). Therefore,
H = \frac{N}{\ell}I
From the above equation, the current \(I\) can be expressed as:
I = \frac{H\ell}{N}
Substitute the values of \(H\), \(\ell\), and \(N\) into the formula:
I = \frac{(2.4 \times 10^3 \text{ A/m}) \times (15 \times 10^{-2} \text{ m})}{60}
I = \frac{2.4 \times 10^3 \times 0.15}{60}
I = \frac{360}{60}
I = 6\text{ A}
The current required is 6 A.

